WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.705 --> 00:00:03.423
One profile write is waiting for a file lock.

2
00:00:03.423 --> 00:00:05.537
Other requests should keep moving.

3
00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:09.645
We ask the heartbeat endpoint a very small question: are you alive?

4
00:00:43.918 --> 00:00:46.425
The answer arrives after the lock timeout.

5
00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:50.363
It is technically alive, and approximately one coffee break late.

6
00:00:50.363 --> 00:00:54.838
A profile-store wait has paused unrelated requests on the same event loop.

7
00:00:57.186 --> 00:01:00.129
An overwrite can fail when a write limit is reached.

8
00:01:00.129 --> 00:01:02.846
A safe upload should leave the old file intact.

9
00:01:02.846 --> 00:01:04.657
Our tiny original is ten bytes.

10
00:01:04.657 --> 00:01:07.601
The attempted replacement is nine hundred thousand.

11
00:01:15.143 --> 00:01:17.351
The server reports file too large.

12
00:01:17.351 --> 00:01:21.117
The original is replaced by half a million partial bytes.

13
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:22.092
Upload failed.

14
00:01:22.092 --> 00:01:25.338
Unfortunately, the old file failed to remain old.

15
00:01:28.007 --> 00:01:29.691
A fake credential is saved.

16
00:01:29.691 --> 00:01:33.372
We temporarily change the encryption key, then restore it.

17
00:01:33.372 --> 00:01:36.054
The first control recovers the credential.

18
00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:40.047
Now repeat the key change, and write one unrelated fake secret.

19
00:01:48.861 --> 00:01:51.115
The unrelated write returns success.

20
00:01:51.115 --> 00:01:55.434
Restore the original encryption key, and our old credential is gone.

21
00:01:55.434 --> 00:01:57.062
The stored value is null.

22
00:01:57.062 --> 00:01:59.941
A side character has erased the hero's lines.

23
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:06.107
Our first character is a filename with one ordinary space.

24
00:02:06.107 --> 00:02:07.112
Git accepts it.

25
00:02:07.112 --> 00:02:11.442
We edit the file, then ask the Agent Server for the list of changes.

26
00:02:21.338 --> 00:02:24.278
A single space produces a four hundred error.

27
00:02:24.278 --> 00:02:27.152
The response contains no changed-file list.

28
00:02:27.152 --> 00:02:28.655
One space, zero lists.

29
00:02:28.655 --> 00:02:32.378
Whitespace has unexpectedly been promoted to lead actor.

30
00:02:34.984 --> 00:02:37.136
Five bytes enter the download path.

31
00:02:37.136 --> 00:02:40.825
The careful courier, File Client, brings back exactly five.

32
00:02:40.825 --> 00:02:43.839
Now Remote Workspace handles the very same file.

33
00:02:47.722 --> 00:02:50.258
Remote Workspace returns eleven bytes.

34
00:02:50.258 --> 00:02:54.262
Three original bytes put on replacement-character costumes.

35
00:02:54.262 --> 00:02:55.796
The parcel got bigger.

36
00:02:55.796 --> 00:02:57.732
The information got smaller.

37
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:03.222
The film shows five defects: three high and two medium.

38
00:03:03.222 --> 00:03:09.542
The full report confirms one hundred thirty groups: twelve high, sixty nine medium, and forty nine low.

39
00:03:09.542 --> 00:03:12.094
Every low severity case is in the report.

40
00:03:12.094 --> 00:03:18.717
The map contains nine hundred thirty four active checks across thirty four families, plus one retired entry.

41
00:03:18.717 --> 00:03:21.695
Three proven cross-family duplicates are merged.
